<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alim Khan of Kokand</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1811">1811</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alim Khan was a ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> who took the title khan around 1800, turning the Kokand state into a khanate. He built a standing army that included Tajik mountain soldiers and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> in 1808 or 1809. His harsh rule made him enemies, and he was killed in 1811 in a revolt that brought his brother Umar to power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Alim Khan came to power after the death of his father, Narbuta Bey, and continued his father's policy of enlarging the state. In 1806 he personally led the campaign to annex Ura-Tepe, but the city resisted so strongly that it had to be taken again several times. He began wide military reforms, which included hiring Tajik mercenary forces, and he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunus-khoja">Yunus Khoja</a>, which his father had attempted without success.</p><p>His conquests also brought in Kurama, with the cities of Ura-Tepe, Jizzak and Khojend, and the formerly independent state of a Kyrgyz sultan that held Shymkent, Turkestan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayram">Sayram</a>. Like Ura-Tepe, these lands resisted several times and were later annexed again under his brother Umar Khan. Along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> a war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakalpaks">Karakalpaks</a> and Kokandian settlers ended with the Karakalpaks driven out to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a>. Alim's strict control and his many unpopular campaigns, among them an unexplained massacre of a Kazakh village in winter, divided his army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His brother Muhammad Umar plotted to remove him by announcing to Alim's forces that the khan had been killed and had named a new governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>. Confused, the 300 troops still loyal to Alim left their position on the Chirchiq river and headed south-east towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>. When forces sworn to Umar took Tashkent and proclaimed him khan, Alim tried to gather his supporters again and named his own son governor of Tashkent.</p><p>Left with only twenty loyal soldiers, Alim was advised to go to Khodjend, where 4,000 reinforcements waited, but insisted on riding to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>. Seventeen of the twenty then abandoned him, and when his horse stuck in the sand he was shot dead by a member of Umar's faction. His son was received with hostility in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> and was soon captured by Umar's forces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alim Khan of Kokand” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Alim Khan</b>, who took the title khan around 1800, created a standing army and conquered Tashkent in 1809.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-kokand">Khanate of Kokand</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alim Khan</b> of the Khanate of Kokand conquered Tashkent in 1808 or 1809, after which it remained a Kokandi city until the Russian conquest of 1865.</blockquote><a href="/state/tashkent-hakimate">Tashkent Hakimate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Qoʻqon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Qoʻqon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.53,70.94&amp;year=1801" aria-label="Qoʻqon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alim Khan of Kokand"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5707 4071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5707 4071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.53,70.94&amp;year=1801">What was here in 1801? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
